{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md'","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\nAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\nSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; the orchestrator's append-only work log is [`docs/orchestrator-log.md`](../orchestrator-log.md).\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md collab/1792524256000-architect-host-boundary-review-r8.gpt-6-astra.findings.md collab/1792524426000-architect-host-boundary-r9.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md collab/1792524426000-engineer-host-boundary-h0-retag2.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md collab/1792524256000-architect-host-boundary-review-r8.gpt-6-astra.findings.md collab/1792524426000-architect-host-boundary-r9.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md collab/1792524426000-engineer-host-boundary-h0-retag2.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"---\nname: code-review-pr\ndescription: >-\n  Performs comprehensive, high-quality code reviews on Git pull requests, branches, commit ranges, or local working tree diffs. Analyzes code correctness, security, architecture, test coverage, and performance using automated diff analysis and structured review checklists.\n---\n\n# Code Review PR Skill\n\n## Overview\n\nThe `code-review-pr` skill provides an end-to-end framework for conducting rigorous, actionable code reviews on Git pull requests, feature branches, commit ranges, or working tree diffs. It combines automated diff scanning via `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` with structured review checklists across security, correctness, architecture, performance, and testing.\n\nUse this skill whenever asked to:\n- Review a pull request or feature branch (`git diff main...HEAD`).\n- Review recent commits (`git diff HEAD~1` or commit range).\n- Review uncommitted local changes (`git diff` or staged changes).\n- Perform a security, quality, or architecture audit of proposed code changes.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\nRun the automated PR reviewer script to generate an initial risk assessment, churn metrics, and automated smell scan:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Review branch changes against main (Default PR review)\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD\n\n# 2. Review last commit\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target HEAD~1\n\n# 3. Review uncommitted working tree changes\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target working\n\n# 4. Save review report to file\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD --output review_report.md\n```\n\n---\n\n## Code Review Workflow\n\nFollow this step-by-step process when executing a code review:\n\n### Step 1: Run Automated Diff Analysis\nExecute `scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze` for the target Git ref/range. Review the output to determine:\n- Total lines added/deleted and file churn distribution.\n- High-risk modified files (e.g., Security/Auth, Database/Schema, Infrastructure, API Contracts).\n- Test-to-source additions ratio.\n- Automated static findings (hardcoded secrets, debug leftovers, swallowed exceptions, SQL injection risks).\n\n### Step 2: Deep-Dive Code Inspection\nInspect the diffs for high-risk files using `git diff <target> -- <path>` or `view_file`. Focus on:\n1. **Logic & Edge Cases**:\n   - Boundary checks, null/nil handling, empty collections, zero division.\n   - Resource leaks (unclosed streams, file handles, DB connections).\n   - Race conditions, shared state mutation, concurrency locks.\n2. **Security & Credentials**:\n   - No hardcoded API keys, passwords, or tokens.\n   - Input validation before execution or query interpolation.\n   - Proper authorization checks on new or updated endpoints.\n3. **Architecture & Contracts**:\n   - Preserving backward compatibility on existing API functions/endpoints.\n   - Proper module separation and clean interface boundaries.\n4. **Test Coverage & Quality**:\n   - Ensuring new source logic is accompanied by unit/integration tests.\n   - Verifying tests validate actual assertions rather than just non-crashing execution.\n\n### Step 3: Reference Checklist & Severity Taxonomy\nConsult [references/review_checklist.md](references/review_checklist.md) to classify findings:\n- **P0 (Critical / Blocker)**: Security breaches, data loss risks, severe crashes, hardcoded credentials.\n- **P1 (High / Must Address)**: Logic bugs, broken API contracts, swallowed exceptions, missing critical tests.\n- **P2 (Medium / Recommended)**: Performance bottlenecks, code duplication, missing parameter validation.\n- **P3 (Low / Suggestion)**: Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs.\n\n### Step 4: Formulate Structured Review Report\nPresent findings to the user using the following template:\n\n```markdown\n# 🔍 Code Review Report\n\n## Executive Summary\n- **Target:** `<git-ref>`\n- **Verdict:** `[ APPROVED | REQUEST CHANGES | COMMENT ]`\n- **Overall Risk:** `[ LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH ]`\n- **Metrics:** `X files changed, +Y / -Z lines`\n\n## 🚨 Critical Findings (P0 / P1)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext:L123` - Short issue description.\n  ```diff\n  - problematic code\n  + suggested fix\n  ```\n\n## 💡 Improvements & Suggestions (P2 / P3)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext` - Recommendation details.\n\n## 🧪 Test Coverage & Quality Assessment\n- Evaluation of test ratio and edge case coverage.\n\n## 🌟 Positive Highlights\n- Clean implementations, thorough documentation, or well-structured refactoring.\n```\n\n---\n\n## Utility Script Reference\n\nThe bundled helper script `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` supports the following options:\n\n| Parameter | Type | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `--target` | String | `main...HEAD` | Git range/ref (`main...HEAD`, `HEAD~1`, `staged`, `working`) |\n| `--output` | String | None | File path to write report output |\n| `--format` | String | `markdown` | Output format (`markdown` or `json`) |\n| `--repo-dir` | String | Current Dir | Path to the target Git repository |\n\n---\n\n## Common Review Pitfalls\n\n1. **Nit Tunnel Vision**: Focusing heavily on formatting or naming while missing underlying logic bugs or edge-case crashes.\n2. **Ignoring Test Ratio**: Approving large source code changes without verifying corresponding test coverage additions.\n3. **Swallowing Symptoms**: Suggesting `try/except` wrappers around failing code rather than addressing root cause invalid inputs or broken state.\n**PART 1 — NOT CONFIRMED**\n\nReferences below use **H** = `docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md`.\n\n- **R7-1 — Not resolved.** The nearest-even algorithm exists, but `integer.cljc:769` defines **private** `int-decimal->float`; `:855` exports a guest-module symbol, not a callable `yin.vm.integer/decimal->float` var. H:640’s proposed call also violates H:678’s prohibition on `dao` requiring `yin`. Reaching it through `integer-module` introduces VM dependencies and, after migration, a cycle through `yin.vm → dao.jing.cbor → dao.host.float`. Specify a dependency-free extraction of the kernel and its helpers. The private `data/code-points` implementation and surrogate tests exist. **76 is honest for Table A’s primitive entries**; portable Table B compositions need not increase it to 78. That count does not excuse the dependency defect.\n- **R7-2 — Partially resolved.** All four changes and consumer acceptance cases are named, including malformed/non-ASCII bytes, exact `9007199254740993`, overflow and canonical carriers. OQ11 correctly remains an **OWNER decision**, preserving the JVM encoding arity. However, H:974–977 still requires all nine consumers to leave list 1 in H2, contradicting OQ11’s retained arm. Explicitly exempt `yin/vm.cljc` pending that decision.\n- **R7-3 — Resolved.** H:460–464 requires canonical comparison for floats on every host; Table D requires signed-zero ties, NaN-last, infinity ordering and `1` versus `1.0`. This preserves the protection documented at `index.cljc:70–85`.\n- **R7-4 — Partially resolved.** Classification edits and regeneration are correctly distinguished from traversal changes. However, H:964’s assertion that the gate checks the category/class mapping is false: it checks category membership, allowed slice and a weak `:none` condition.\n- **R7-5 — Resolved in the design.** Revision chronology, §4.2 reference and exclusion of `flush` from the 13 counted prints are corrected. Ringbuffer now has a consistent seams placement in r8, which the retag has not adopted.\n\n**H1 commission: NO, pending the decimal-kernel dependency correction and reconciliation above.** H1 must build the five `dao.host.{integer,float,number,bytes,text}` files, both Table B functions (`code-points`, `decimal-text->float`) and their direct tests, plus strict UTF-8 encoding and mutation-prevalidation laws and tests.\n\n**PART 2 — SIGNED OFF WITH CHANGES**\n\nVerified base `9f6fc27d` and the five-file diff. The 16 entries carry r7’s **9 migration/H2, 6 dialect/stays, 1 deferred/stays** assignments. Their source sites support the migration/deferred distinctions; I found no evidence of categories invented merely to hide arms. R8 changes the required ringbuffer placement.\n\nHere **G** = `.claude/worktrees/host-h0/test/dao/host/gate_test.clj`.\n\n| ID | Severity | File:line | Evidence | Correction |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| H0-1 | Medium | G:514 | Ringbuffer’s atom test is labelled dialect despite that category’s display/EDN definition and r8’s seams row. | Use `:seam-driver`, retaining `:stays`. |\n| H0-2 | Medium | G:487 | `\"H2\"` means removal, but OQ11 requires a surviving JVM encoding arm. | Record conditional retention pending OWNER resolution; reconcile the design’s removal rule. |\n| H0-3 | Medium | G:708–714 | Unknown categories and row-disallowed slices fail the test suite. Classes are collapsed across lanes, so a later lane can mask `#{:none}`; category/class compatibility is never checked. | Validate each lane’s nonempty class set, reject `:none`, and enforce the documented mapping. |\n\nWaitset consolidation and ringbuffer classification are legitimate. Removing `lane-rows` slightly changes classification code, **not traversal**. Closure diffs change tags/legends only. Both injection tests and the scan-failure test retain their original semantics.\n\n**Could not verify:** I did not independently rerun the reported 293 assertions, closure check, corpus hashes or runtime lanes. No files were edited.\nCreated-GMT: 2026-10-20 20:35:00 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-21 03:35:00 +07\nCoding-Agent: claude\nModel: claude-fable-5-1\nSession-ID: 36134bec-9cbd-4852-b78c-4901af66fd02\nTask: host-boundary design r9, astra's review of r8 (R7-1 not resolved; R7-2 and R7-4 partial; H0-1 to H0-3)\nRole: Architect\nDeliverable: docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md edited in place (r9); no src edits, nothing staged.\nLabels: V verified by reading the cited lines. No lane run. A non-Claude reviewer must confirm r9 before H1 is dispatched.\n\n# Rationale\n\n**R7-1, resolved by extraction.** Astra is right on both counts: the\nnearest-even kernel is the private `int-decimal->float`\n(`integer.cljc:769`), the `:855` export is a guest-module symbol, and a\n`dao` namespace calling into `yin` would break the layering and, after H2,\nclose a cycle. Reading the kernel shows every helper is written over the\nhost shim alone (`sign`, `bits`, `pow2`, `signed`, `ratio->float`,\n`trunc-big`, `digits->big`, `pow10`, `scaled`, `float-ratio`,\n`decimal-exponent`, V), so Table B now specifies a dependency-free\nextraction: `dao.host.float` (over `dao.host.integer`, no `yin` require)\nowns `decimal->float` (digit values, exponent, any carrier, to a double;\n`+inf`/`0.0` past the range; never refuses), `float-digits`, `ratio->float`\nand the helpers, with exact signatures and laws; `yin.vm.integer` keeps its\nrefusal preambles and delegates, so one implementation exists; `yang.php`\nis the second consumer through the `decimal-text->float` composition.\nCounting: none of it is in the 76 (no arm); it is one Table B item. Oracle:\nH1 runs the existing float fixture rows directly against `dao.host.float`,\nH2 runs the unchanged module suites through the rewiring. `code-points` is\nconfirmed callable from `dao.host` without `yin` (it uses only the two\ntext arms) and its surrogate cases exist.\n\n**R7-2.** `yin/vm.cljc` is exempt from H2's removal pending OQ11: after H2\nits one-arity is portable and the JVM two-arity keeps one arm, so it stays\non list 1 as `:migration-source` with the slice value `:pending` (\"OQ11\"),\na new value in the gate's `:clears` vocabulary (astra's H0-2: `\"H2\"` means\nremoval). The resolution paths are stated.\n\n**R7-4.** The false \"the gate asserts the mapping\" sentence is replaced by\nthe precise category-to-class table and the stronger check H0's retag\nimplements: per-lane class sets, non-empty, never `:none`, intersecting\nthe category's permitted classes, never collapsed across lanes (H0-3).\nRing buffer: `:seam-driver`, `:stays` (H0-1).\n\nScope, owner decisions and structure are unchanged; §14 states what H1\nmust build beyond r7 (the extracted kernel, `code-points`,\n`decimal-text->float`, their tests).\n\n# Disposition\n\n+-------+-----------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Id    | Result    | Where in r9                                                                                    |\n+=======+===========+================================================================================================+\n| R7-1  | accepted  | Table B \"float-text kernel, extracted\" (owner, moved functions, signatures, laws, consumers,   |\n|       |           | counting, oracle, slices); `code-points` dependency note; table D row; H2 cell; §14; header.   |\n+-------+-----------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| R7-2  | accepted  | §8.1 \"How H1 and H2 clear the tag\": `yin/vm.cljc` exempt, `:pending` value, resolution paths.  |\n+-------+-----------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| R7-4  | accepted  | §8.1 \"The category-to-class mapping, stated\": table and the strengthened per-lane check.       |\n+-------+-----------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| H0-1  | accepted  | §8.1: ring buffer `:seam-driver`, `:stays`.                                                    |\n+-------+-----------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| H0-2  | accepted  | §8.1: `:pending` slice value for conditionally retained arms.                                  |\n+-------+-----------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| H0-3  | accepted  | §8.1: per-lane class sets, no collapse, `:none` rejected per lane.                             |\n+-------+-----------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\nCreated-GMT: 2026-10-20 19:40:00 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-21 02:40:00 +07\nCoding-Agent: claude\nModel: claude-opus-5-5\nRole: QA & Verification engineer\nPrompt: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792524426000-engineer-host-boundary-h0-retag2.claude-opus-5-5.prompt.md\nReview addressed: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792524256000-architect-host-boundary-review-r8.gpt-6-astra.findings.md (Part 2, H0-1..H0-3)\nDesign read: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md (r8: §3.1, §8.1 rows and the reconciliation paragraph, §13 OQ11)\nWorktree: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/host-h0 (branch host-h0, uncommitted on top of 9f6fc27d)\nStatus: DONE. All three review findings are fixed. Nothing is staged, committed or pushed.\n\n# H0 retag, round 2: astra's findings H0-1, H0-2 and H0-3\n\nThis round changes only `test/dao/host/gate_test.clj`. The closure tool,\nthe closure edn files, the corpus hashes and all `src/` code are unchanged\nfrom round 1; the tool's `--check` and the corpus `--check` both exit 0.\n\n## H0-1: the ring buffer is now in the seams row\n\n`src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc` moved from `:dialect-arm` to\n**`:seam-driver`, `:stays`**. Its reason reads: \":168 the in-memory stream\nimplementation's own host atom test (§4.1, implementation-private)\".\n\nr8's §8.1 seams row names it, and its closure class is already\n`#{:deferred-entropy :external-stream-seam}`, which the seams category\nadmits.\n\nThe dialect row now holds the five files r8 lists. The category counts\nare now:\n\n| Category | Files |\n|---|---|\n| migration-source | 24 |\n| seam-driver | 22 |\n| deferred-scope | 16 |\n| dialect-arm | 5 |\n| codec-track | 5 |\n| runtime-prereq | 3 |\n| profile | 1 |\n| **Total** | **76** |\n\n## H0-2: `yin/vm.cljc` is retained conditionally, pending OQ11\n\n- `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc` now clears with the new value\n  **`:conditional-retention-oq11`**. Its reason: H2 migrates the one-arity\n  `bytes->str` to strict UTF-8 (a named behaviour change), while the JVM\n  encoding arity stays behind a retained arm until OQ11 is decided.\n- The value is documented in the `list-1-categories` docstring and is\n  allowed **only** in the `:migration-source` row's `:clears` set.\n- The other eight profile consumers keep `\"H2\"`.\n\n## H0-3: validation now runs per lane and checks the category-to-class mapping\n\n- **The mapping is data.** Each row in `list-1-categories` now has\n  `:classes`: the §3.1 closure classes a file in that row may carry. The\n  seams row also has `:lane-specific-classes`, which only `.clj`, `.cljs`\n  and `.cljd` files may use instead (see the table below).\n- **The checks are a pure function.** `(list-1-problems list-1\n  lane-classes)` returns `[kind file detail]` triples. The input is\n  `{lane {file classes}}`, built per lane from the committed edn by\n  `committed-lane-classes`. Lanes are checked one at a time and never\n  merged. The problem kinds are:\n  - `:unknown-category`\n  - `:slice-not-allowed` (the slice is not in that row's `:clears`)\n  - `:in-no-lane`\n  - for each lane whose closure holds the file:\n    - `:empty-classes`\n    - `:class-none` (the class set is exactly `#{:none}`)\n    - `:class-not-in-category` (no class the category admits)\n- **The real data has no problems.** The test asserts\n  `(= [] (list-1-problems list-1 (committed-lane-classes)))`.\n- **Each check is shown to fail.** The same test has seven in-memory\n  negative cases, each on a deliberately wrong entry:\n  - category `:h0-unlisted` gives `#{:unknown-category}`;\n  - slice `\"H9\"` gives `#{:slice-not-allowed}`;\n  - `:conditional-retention-oq11` on `dao/pretty.cljc` (runtime-prereq\n    row) gives `#{:slice-not-allowed}`;\n  - the ring buffer recategorised to `:dialect-arm` gives\n    `#{:class-not-in-category}`;\n  - `yin/vm.cljc` set to `#{:none}` on **cljs only**, with clj and cljd\n    untouched, gives exactly\n    `[[:class-none \"src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc\" [:cljs :migration-source #{:none}]]]`,\n    so another lane's tags cannot hide it;\n  - `#{}` on cljd only gives exactly the one `:empty-classes` problem;\n  - a list-1 file that no lane holds gives `#{:in-no-lane}`.\n\nThe mapping, derived from §3.1 and §8.1 of r8. Where the design was\nambiguous I implemented the narrowest checkable set; the ambiguities are\nlisted below.\n\n| Category | Admitted closure classes |\n|---|---|\n| `:migration-source` | `:profile-internal-pure`, `:profile-hash-sign`, `:profile-consumer` |\n| `:profile` | `:profile-internal-pure` |\n| `:runtime-prereq` | `:runtime-prereq`, `:deferred-storage`, `:deferred-timers`, `:internal-pure-display` |\n| `:codec-track` | `:codec-track` |\n| `:dialect-arm` | `:dialect-arm` |\n| `:seam-driver` | `:external-stream-seam`, `:driver`; lane-specific files may instead carry `:deferred-stage`, `:deferred-timers` or `:codec-track` |\n| `:deferred-scope` | `:deferred-storage`, `:deferred-entropy`, `:deferred-timers`, `:deferred-stage`, `:deferred-gensym`, `:runtime-prereq` |\n\n## Kept unchanged\n\n- Totals 142/140/141 with 133 shared.\n- The traversal and its independent agreement test.\n- The two injection tests and the scan-failure test.\n- The corpus hash set.\n- list 2.\n- The closure tool and its edn outputs, which round 1 last changed.\n\n## Results (all run in the foreground in the worktree)\n\n```\n$ clojure -M:test -n dao.host.gate-test\nRan 10 tests containing 73 assertions.\n0 failures, 0 errors.\n\n$ clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:hb ...}}' -M:hb -m host-boundary.closure --check\nclj 142 / cljs 140 / cljd 141, shared 133   -> exit 0\n\n$ bb src/dev/host_boundary/corpus_hashes.clj --check\n18 files; corpus identical                  -> exit 0\n```\n\nclj-kondo reports 0 errors and 0 warnings. cljstyle check is clean.\n\n**Assertion count.** The count went from 293 in round 1 to **73**: the\noriginal 65, plus 1 aggregate `list-1-problems` assertion, plus 7\nnegative cases. It went down because round 1's 228 per-file `is` calls\nwere replaced by one assertion over the full problem vector. That vector\nnames every offending file, lane and category when it fails, so no\ndiagnostic is lost.\n\nThe test count is still 10, as no deftest was added. The derived JVM-lane\ntotal is 4131 tests and 286103 - 65 + 73 = **286111** assertions. I did\nnot re-run the full lane.\n\n## Ambiguities in the design (r8)\n\n1. **The seams row versus §3.1 for lane-specific files.** §8.1's seams row\n   takes every lane-specific file except the Dart Jing writer, whatever\n   that file's §3.1 row is. The `yang.antlr.cst` files are\n   `:deferred-stage`, `content/driver.clj` is `:deferred-timers`, and the\n   cbor/transit Dart twins are `:codec-track`. I admitted those three\n   classes **only for lane-specific files**, not for `.cljc` files in the\n   row.\n2. **`:runtime-prereq` admits deferred and display classes.** §8.1 puts\n   `btree/storage.cljc` (§3.1: deferred storage and timers, \"promise\n   facade, deferred §9.1\") and `dao/pretty.cljc` (§3.1: its own\n   internal-pure display row) in the table C row. I admitted\n   `:deferred-storage`, `:deferred-timers` and `:internal-pure-display`\n   there. A narrower design would give `pretty` a \"display\" category of\n   its own.\n3. **`:deferred-scope` admits `:runtime-prereq`.** §8.1 lists\n   `transactor.cljc` and `ucf/authority.cljc` (`locking`) in the deferred\n   row, but §3.1 classes `locking` as table C (`:runtime-prereq`), and\n   those two files carry nothing else.\n4. **`sign.cljc` clears as `:stays`.** r8 says H3 migrates it but it stays\n   for `generate`. It keeps `:stays` (round 1's choice), not a\n   conditional value like OQ11's, because its retention is unconditional\n   (deferred entropy).\n5. **The check requires overlap, not that every class maps.** A file\n   passes if it has *at least one* admitted class on each lane, not if\n   *every* class it has maps to its category. Many files sit in several\n   §3.1 rows: `yin.repl` is driver and entropy, `journal` is entropy and\n   table C. Requiring every class to match would contradict r8's\n   \"many-to-one\" wording and §3.1's multi-row files.\n\nNot done:\n- no design-doc edit;\n- no `src/` behaviour change;\n- no `deps.edn` or `bb.edn` change;\n- no full-lane run;\n- no stage, commit or push.\n\nReport: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792524426000-engineer-host-boundary-h0-retag2.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
